"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:83554378db55a884fd202dba0dff365118fed606d42575d87051257aeae1942c", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:22dd1b3c654adcf14ae86080b9c3e6d6dfdb3feb852abceeffd1c94e40854d1f", "source_text": "Quotient stacks<FOOTNOTEOPEN_0001>In the literature, <EMPHOPEN_0001>quotient stack<EMPHCLOSE_0001> often means a stack of the form <MATH_0001> with <MATH_0002> an algebraic space and <MATH_0003> a subgroup scheme of <MATH_0004> rather than an arbitrary flat group scheme.<FOOTNOTECLOSE_0001> form a very important subclass of Artin stacks which include almost all moduli stacks studied by algebraic geometers. The geometry of a quotient stack <MATH_0005> is the <MATH_0006>-equivariant geometry of <MATH_0007>. It is often easier to show properties are true for quotient stacks and some results are only known to be true for quotient stacks. The following papers address: When is an algebraic stack a global quotient stack? Is an algebraic stack <QUOTEOPEN_0001>locally<QUOTECLOSE_0001> a quotient stack?", "source_text_hash": "sha256:395c81a10ce8ee7d0b4e390b02043981119b83c7d11599d5d33448173ba25a57", "unit_id": "tag:04UZ:p001"}
