"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:5962d36c0032981ddff4cce60ce565bfbeec936e5584e64ed92505aacd347f67", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:7f9ce2cc61bd33eaaa066e1a85e54b7fc45bb8972ad79384c7a6e44e56c21736", "source_text": "Chapter 6 contains several facts about the local and global structure of algebraic stacks. It is proved that an algebraic stack <MATH_0001> over <MATH_0002> is a quotient stack <MATH_0003> with <MATH_0004> an algebraic space (resp. scheme, resp. affine scheme) and <MATH_0005> a finite group if and only if there exists an algebraic space (resp. scheme, resp. affine scheme) <MATH_0006> and an finite <LATIN_0001> morphism <MATH_0007>. It is shown that any Deligne-Mumford stack over <MATH_0008> and <MATH_0009> admits an representable, <LATIN_0002> and separated morphism <MATH_0010> where <MATH_0011> is a finite group acting on an affine scheme over <MATH_0012> such that <MATH_0013>. The existence of presentations with geometrically connected fibers is also discussed in detail.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:51c9fbbec7d355b56ed4093801cd0a911d8afcc01d79d9229d0cc966895b7299", "unit_id": "tag:04UZ:item001-quote"}
