"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:1a57072e2e1690fed4ba3211c04d5e47293951c3c8524dd2bb14be7297d3503e", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:73a0d404e9ee13cbd9463b00619629df6e36cac0d23c603a169362bd8eb729d2", "source_text": "First, they establish some fundamental (although not very difficult) facts concerning when a given algebraic stack (always assumed finite type over a Noetherian scheme in this paper) is a quotient stack. For an algebraic stack <MATH_0001> : <MATH_0002> is a quotient stack if and only if there exists a vector bundle <MATH_0003> such that for every geometric point, the stabilizer acts faithfully on the fiber if and only if there exists a vector bundle <MATH_0004> and a locally closed substack <MATH_0005> such that <MATH_0006> is representable and surjects onto <MATH_0007>. They establish that an algebraic stack is a quotient stack if there exists finite flat cover by an algebraic space. Any smooth Deligne-Mumford stack with generically trivial stabilizer is a quotient stack. They show that a <MATH_0008>-gerbe over a Noetherian scheme <MATH_0009> corresponding to <MATH_0010> is a quotient stack if and only if <MATH_0011> is in the image of the Brauer map <MATH_0012>. They use this to produce a non-separated Deligne-Mumford stack that is not a quotient stack.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:1d092fa2fdd3100eb044a8057d005656f4965780eb8bc0fbcae3e3178795a459", "unit_id": "tag:04UZ:item002-quote"}
