"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:3c058d293959b187301f5d9d799a97663e22832b0671ec3dabfdaeb4f6c7b58e", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:eb2f8500b087a301f12efa20876a2c3701cb331fd3b37ddcae80bf0ecae36405", "source_text": "It is conjectured that for an Artin stack <MATH_0001> and a closed point <MATH_0002> with linearly reductive stabilizer, then there is an <LATIN_0001> morphism <MATH_0003> with <MATH_0004> an algebraic space. Some evidence for this conjecture is given. A simple deformation theory argument (based on ideas in <CITE_0001>) shows that it is true formally locally. A stack-theoretic proof of Luna's <LATIN_0002> slice theorem is presented proving that for stacks <MATH_0005> with <MATH_0006> linearly reductive, then <LATIN_0003> locally on the GIT quotient <MATH_0007>, <MATH_0008> is a quotient stack by the stabilizer.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:fb0b017f915069b23ae2807353eb80c32674e278310b73600a24335f89770ea4", "unit_id": "tag:04UZ:item009-quote"}

